### Design Decisions & Constraints

- **No DMZ:** Meridian hosts no internet-facing servers. All 
  customer-facing services (online banking, core) are vendor-hosted by 
  Fiserv. Perimeter exposure is limited to VPN termination points.
- **SD-WAN over traditional IPsec site-to-site:** with a 1-person network 
  admin supporting two states, centralized management of the WAN was 
  prioritized over cost of dedicated circuits.
- **Backup internet circuit at HQ only:** acceptable risk — the Manhattan 
  branch can operate "offline mode" (paper + queued transactions) briefly 
  during an outage.
- **Guest Wi-Fi (VLAN 99) is internet-only,** with firewall rules 
  preventing any routing to internal VLANs.
- **Cameras and VoIP share VLAN 30:** a deliberate trade-off to conserve 
  switch ports and PoE budget. Documented as an accepted risk pending 
  future segmentation (see Chapter 08 gap analysis).
- **Backups are local-first with immutable copies,** given Fiserv owns the 
  core; the backup server is the one system the bank fully controls for 
  recovery from a core-adjacent incident.
- **VLAN numbering is standardized across sites** (20 = workstations, 
  30 = VoIP/cameras, 99 = guest) to simplify administration for a 
  one-person network team.
